---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { EQUIPMENT } from "../data/equipment";
import { SOURCES } from "../data/sources";
import { CATEGORIES } from "../data/types";
import { u } from "../lib/url";

const claims = EQUIPMENT.flatMap((e) => e.signboard);
const byVerdict = {
  supported: claims.filter((c) => c.verdict === "supported").length,
  partly: claims.filter((c) => c.verdict === "partly").length,
  unsupported: claims.filter((c) => c.verdict === "unsupported").length,
};
const sources = Object.values(SOURCES);
---

<Base
  title="About Open Gym"
  description="What this guide is, how the recommendations and claim audits were produced, what the data licence is, and what it deliberately does not do."
  path="/about"
>
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="padding-top:22px">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>An honest manual for a machine with no manual</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      India installed outdoor gyms at scale and shipped them without instructions. The result is a
      park full of equipment that people use by imitation, on frames nobody inspects, guided by
      plaques that promise things exercise cannot deliver. Open Gym is an attempt to write the
      missing manual — openly, and with the evidence showing.
    </p>

    <div class="hero__strip" style="margin-top:22px">
      <div class="hero__stat"><strong>{EQUIPMENT.length}</strong><span>stations</span></div>
      <div class="hero__stat"><strong>{CATEGORIES.length}</strong><span>categories</span></div>
      <div class="hero__stat"><strong>{claims.length}</strong><span>claims audited</span></div>
      <div class="hero__stat"><strong>{sources.length}</strong><span>sources</span></div>
    </div>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>What is in here</h2>
      <p>
        Every station entry answers the same questions in the same order: what the machine is, which
        muscles it loads, how to set it up, how to move on it, the mistakes that cause most injuries,
        what it is genuinely good for, how long to spend, what overuse looks like, what to inspect,
        how to progress, and what the park board gets wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        Illustrations are hand-drawn SVG schematics rather than photographs. They render sharply at
        any width, work offline, and carry no copyright or model-release problems — which matters for
        a guide meant to be copied and printed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>How the claim audit works</h2>
      <p>
        Indian park equipment routinely carries plaques claiming waist reduction, height increase,
        fat burning from specific body parts, and cures for back pain. We collected
        {claims.length} such claims and graded each one:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Count</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict verdict--supported">Supported</span></td><td>{byVerdict.supported}</td><td>The station plausibly delivers this, within limits stated in the entry.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict verdict--partly">Partly true</span></td><td>{byVerdict.partly}</td><td>Directionally right, but overstated or conditional — the entry says where the limit is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="verdict verdict--unsupported">Not supported</span></td><td>{byVerdict.unsupported}</td><td>False, or no plausible mechanism. Marked even when the station itself is useful.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:10px">
        A “not supported” verdict is about the claim, not the machine. The sit-up board is a fine
        place to train trunk flexion; it still does not remove belly fat.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>How recommendations are built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sets and repetitions</strong> follow novice resistance-training guidance: 2–3 sets of 8–12 at moderate effort, with 48 hours between hard sessions on the same muscle group.</li>
        <li><strong>Weekly volume</strong> is framed against the WHO guideline — 150–300 minutes of moderate activity plus two muscle-strengthening days for adults; 60 minutes a day on average for ages 5–17.</li>
        <li><strong>Children's bands</strong> come from equipment standards, not fitness enthusiasm: EN 16630 covers adult and youth fitness equipment, EN 1176 covers playground fall height and entrapment. A park gym is the former.</li>
        <li><strong>Overuse patterns</strong> follow load-management principles: tendon pain tracks load spikes, and shoulder positions at end-range are the ones that provoke impingement.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted">
        The times on each page are programming suggestions, not prescriptions. They assume a healthy
        adult who has not trained much. Start lower than you think you need.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Confidence, and what it means</h2>
      <p>
        Each station carries a confidence label. It reflects how much direct evidence exists for
        that specific machine — not how safe it is, and not how strongly we feel about it.
      </p>
      <dl class="deflist">
        <div><dt>High</dt><dd>The movement pattern and the station are well described in standard guidance or equipment literature.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Moderate</dt><dd>The pattern is well understood, but the specific station or its typical Indian installation is less documented.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Low</dt><dd>Limited direct evidence; we say so rather than inventing certainty.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>This is not medical advice, physiotherapy, or a substitute for being taught a movement by a person.</li>
        <li>It does not know your body. A station marked safe for most adults can be wrong for you.</li>
        <li>The park registry starts from walks, not surveys. Unverified entries are snapshots and equipment changes.</li>
        <li>Equipment varies. Two “chest press” stations from different manufacturers can have different pivots, ranges and loads. The entry describes the type, not your specific frame.</li>
        <li>It is India-focused in context — municipal maintenance, heat, monsoon, park culture — but the exercise guidance is drawn from international sources.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Data and licence</h2>
      <p>
        The full catalogue is downloadable as <a href={u("/data/equipment.json")}>JSON</a>,
        <a href={u("/data/equipment.csv")}>CSV</a> and
        <a href={u("/data/equipment.duckdb")}>DuckDB</a> (tables: equipment, benefits,
        signboard_claims, parks). Content is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0; the code is MIT.
        Reuse it, translate it, print it for a park noticeboard — attribute it and keep the licence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Corrections are the point. If a claim audit is wrong, a time is unsafe, or a station is
        missing, open an issue on
        <a href="https://github.com/LogicIncZo/open-gym">GitHub</a> with the station id and the source.
        Contested entries get the disagreement written into the page rather than quietly edited out.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="sources">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <span class="src-label"><a href={s.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.label}</a></span>
            <span class="src-meta">{s.publisher} · {s.year} · {s.tier}</span>
            {s.note && <span class="tiny muted">{s.note}</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Who made it</h2>
      <p>
        Open Gym is a LogicIncZo public-good project, built to be forked and localised. It has no
        accounts, no analytics and no advertising. The whole park-builder runs in your browser: the
        inventory travels in the link, and nothing is stored.
      </p>
      <div class="row">
        <a class="btn btn--terra" href={u("/equipment")}>Browse stations</a>
        <a class="btn" href={u("/build")}>Build a park</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
